    {
      "title": "Abhijeet Dipke, Neha Bora detained on way to 'Lehra Do Tiranga' protest",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/india-news/abhijeet-dipke-neha-bora-detained-on-way-to-lehra-do-tiranga-protest-126101601015_1.html",
      "description": "Cockroach Janta Party (CJP) leader Abhijeet Dipke and AISA chief Neha Bohra were detained by Delhi Police on Friday while on their way to Connaught Place to participate in the 'Lehra Do Tiranga' protest in Connaught Place.\nWhile Dipke was picked up from his home in Jhandewalan, Bohra was detained in Mandi House, both in Central Delhi. There was chaos with jostling crowds, camera crews and swarms of security personnel as the two were detained.\nEarlier in the day, more than 20 Delhi Police personnel visited Dipke's residence in Jhandewalan and served him a notice directing him to refrain from any action that could prejudice the rights of citizens, traders, commuters, patients and others.\nThe police action comes amid heightened security arrangements in the national capital ahead of the protest on Friday evening.",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-10-16T12:23:20"
    },
    {
      "title": "Delhi police serves notice to Abhijeet Dipke ahead of CJP protest today",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/india-news/protests-don-t-hurt-businesses-govt-restrictions-do-cjp-s-abhijit-dipke-126101600544_1.html",
      "description": "Businesses do not suffer because of peaceful protests but when the government shuts down an entire city, suspends internet and restricts citizens' movement, Cockroach Janta Party (CJP) founder Abhijit Dipke said on Friday.\nIn a post on X, Dipke defended the party's commitment to peace and non-violence ahead of its proposed 'Lehra Do Tiranga' march at Central Park in Connaught Place.\nThe CJP had organised protests in Mumbai, Pune, Jaipur, Lucknow, Bengaluru and Nagpur, and all of them were peaceful, he said.\nDipke said he was personally assaulted in Jaipur but chose not to retaliate, since responding to violence with violence went against the movement's principles.\n\"We would rather exercise the rights guaranteed to us by the Constitution, drafted under the leadership of Dr B R Ambedkar, than resort to physical force,\" he said.\nRejecting attempts to portray CJP members as anarchists, Dipke said demanding answers from the government, protesting against injustice and questioning publi",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-10-16T08:27:54"
    },
